			<div class="content" id="post-content-2094109">
									

<p>Once you drop off the continental shelf, it pretty rapidly goes down to around 8,000 to 10,000 feet, but that's only 3&#160;km deep. You are correct in that there is no part of the ocean around there that is that deep.</p>
<p>What actually stuck out to me more was the description of 2120-02; a <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clipper">clipper</a> was a specific type of sailing vessel and one of the last types of ships built during the Age of Sail. Not only does it make no sense for there to be any kind of power source for a clipper (and thus there should be no source of fuel), they were never militarized. They were fast, narrow ships built for hauling small cargoes of very valuable goods (such as silver or tea) very quickly; their design would not have been useful for naval warfare by any means.</p>
<p>If -02 was merely a ship resembling a clipper, or if it had clearly been modified for warfare use, then that should receive special mention in the article. As it stands, it reads like something written by someone who has no clue what a clipper is.</p>

